## Chapter 11: Inside Vault 3
The heavy steel door of Vault 3 closed with a muffled, hydraulic thud, shutting out the biting fog of the rooftop and the distant hum of the city.
The air down here smelled entirely of hot lubricant, ozone, and decades of rusted metal. The vault was a massive subterranean cavern filled with twenty-foot piles of unsorted military and industrial scrap: cracked power cores, bent shock-batons, shattered exo-frames, and dried monster chitin stacked in reinforced wooden crates.
Master Silas walked over to a cluttered workbench littered with dismounted plasma torches and diagnostic slates. He picked up a rag, wiped a smudge of black grease from his palm, and turned around to look at the two students.
"The inventory manifest from the municipal dump is three days late," Silas rasped, pointing a greasy wrench toward the far corner of the vault. "Over there—Section 4. Three crates of raw chitin from the Sector 7 clearing. I need the salvaged plates separated by density grade. High-density plate goes into the steel bins. Crumbly trash goes into the incinerator chute."
Renna unslung her heavy tower shield, setting it against a worktable with a loud metal clatter. "Yes, Master Silas. We'll get on it."
"Get to work," Silas muttered, turning back to his workbench.
Kaelen stayed silent, offering a soft, obedient nod. He followed Renna toward Section 4, keeping his steps slightly heavy and letting his shoulders slump into his usual timid posture.
The crates in Section 4 were three feet tall, constructed from thick, reinforced timber. Inside lay dozens of jagged, jet-black chitin plates torn from **Iron-Claw Raptors** and **Grave-Crawlers**.
Renna leaned over the first crate, picking up a heavy, curved carapace plate. She squinted at it, tapping it with her knuckle.
"How are we supposed to tell the density grade without an aetheric scanner?" Renna muttered, frustration evident in her voice. "It all looks like dirty black rock to me."
Kaelen knelt beside the open box. He didn't need an aetheric scanner. His **[Low-Light Spatial Perception]** and his baseline **24 Aetheric Resonance** allowed him to see the faint, microscopic structural fractures inside the material instantly.
He reached into the crate, his bare fingers lightly brushing against a heavy piece of obsidian hide.
*This piece is hollowed out by internal acid decay—trash,* Kaelen analyzed in a fraction of a second. He picked it up with a deliberate, faked effort, letting out a small grunt as he tossed it into the incinerator bin.
He reached back in and picked up a smaller, lighter-looking piece of dull grey bone. To an untrained eye, it looked like brittle scrap. But Kaelen's sense picked up the tightly woven, shock-absorbent mineral weave beneath the surface—a prime piece of sub-dermal plating.
He quietly set it into the steel bin.
For twenty minutes, the only sound in Vault 3 was the rhythmic clatter of metal and bone as they worked. Renna took five minutes on every piece, carefully examining the edges, while Kaelen tossed scrap with quiet, rapid precision.
Over at the workbench, Silas wasn't working on his plasma torch.
The old mechanic had his brass magnifying goggles turned down, his grey eyes locked onto Kaelen's hands through the reflection of a polished copper mirror resting on his bench.
Silas watched the way Kaelen sorted the crates. The kid didn't check the weight twice. He didn't use a measuring gauge. He simply touched a piece of chitin for half a second and separated high-grade military material from useless ash with a 100% accuracy rate that a seasoned master smith would envy.
*He isn't guessing,* Silas thought, his eyes narrowing behind the brass lenses. *He knows the internal grain of the material before he even lifts it.*
Silas set his wrench down with a deliberate, loud *BANG*.
Renna jumped slightly, turning around. "Master Silas?"
Silas pushed his goggles up onto his forehead, stepped away from his workbench, and walked over to their sorting station. He stopped right beside Kaelen, his shadow falling over the open crate.
He reached down, picked up the dull grey piece of bone Kaelen had just placed into the high-density bin, and held it up to the fluorescent light.
"You put this in the steel bin, kid," Silas said, his voice dropping into a low, dangerous gravel. "It looks like dried chalk. Why isn't it in the fire chute?"
Renna looked over, frowning. "Kaelen, is that high-grade? It looks pretty brittle..."
Kaelen didn't freeze. His **[Hive-Mind Synapse]** processed Silas's posture and tone instantly, identifying zero immediate hostile threat—only deep, probing curiosity.
Kaelen looked up, making his eyes look wide, slightly nervous, and uncertain. He rubbed the back of his neck with a dirty hand.
"I... I just noticed the grain on the edge, sir," Kaelen stammered meekly, pointing a finger at a tiny, microscopic ridge near the fracture point. "When I was cleaning scrap in Sector 9, the older workers told me that if the bone grain runs parallel like a wire rope, it absorbs shock better than thick plate, even if the outside looks chalky."
Silence fell over the vault.
Renna looked between Kaelen and the piece of bone, surprised. "Wait, really?"
Silas stared down at Kaelen for three long seconds. Then, without a word, the old man pulled a heavy iron hammer from his belt loop, laid the grey bone flat on the anvil table, and brought the hammer down with full force.
*CLANG!*
A bright spark erupted. The iron hammer bounced off the grey bone, leaving not a single crack or dent on its surface. It was compact, shock-absorbent alloy-grade material.
Renna let out a low whistle. "Whoa."
Silas lowered his hammer. He looked at the bone, then turned his gaze back to Kaelen. The grim, knowing smirk returned to the corner of his mouth.
"Parallel grain," Silas repeated quietly, tapping the hammer against his palm. "They don't teach that in the standard textbook until second year at Apex Central."
"I... I just got lucky reading old salvage manuals, Master Silas," Kaelen muttered, lowering his head and pulling his sleeves down over his wrists.
"Sure you did, kid," Silas rasped, his voice dripping with dry sarcasm. He turned toward Renna. "Girl, go down to Bay 2 and fetch the heavy hydraulic jack. The lifting rig is stuck."
"Right away, sir," Renna said, wiping sweat from her forehead as she headed down the side corridor.
The moment Renna's footsteps faded out of earshot, the atmosphere in Vault 3 shifted.
Silas leaned against the anvil table, crossing his arms over his chest. He looked directly into Kaelen's eyes, no longer hiding his scrutiny.
"You've got good hands, Vance," Silas said calmly. "Too good for a kid who claims he barely survived a sewer clearing with a rented crowbar."
Kaelen kept his slouched posture, keeping his expression meek. "I just try to be careful, sir."
"Careful?" Silas let out a dry, raspy chuckle. "A careful Salvager doesn't smell like fresh Matriarch acid. A careful Salvager doesn't sort alloy chitin by touch in two seconds flat. And a careful Salvager certainly doesn't stand with his center of mass locked so tight that a charging Raptor couldn't knock him over."
Kaelen didn't answer. He remained still, his face an unreadable mask of timid confusion.
Silas waved his hand dismissively. "Relax, kid. I'm not a municipal inspector, and I don't give a damn about Horgan's rules. I've been in this scrap yard for forty years. I know a wild predator when I smell one."
The old mechanic stood up straight, tossing the gray bone piece into Kaelen's hands. Kaelen caught it effortlessly, his fingers wrapping around the material without making a sound.
"The Joint Sector Assessment is in two weeks," Silas said, his grey eyes turning dead serious. "Ironclad is bringing heavy kinetic gear. Gideon is bringing corporate-enchanted weapons. Anvil Ridge is going in with rusted shields and cardboard armor."
Silas stepped closer, leaning in so his voice didn't echo toward the corridor.
"If you want to keep playing the broke, useless Salvager to the rest of the world, that's your business," Silas whispered. "But if you need raw, unrefined high-density materials to build something that keeps you and your squad alive in that zone... Vault 3's back door doesn't have a security camera."
Kaelen looked down at the gray bone in his palm, then raised his eyes to meet Silas's gaze.
For the very first time since entering Anvil Ridge, Kaelen didn't lower his posture. He didn't drop his eyes. He stood up straight, his slouched shoulders squaring off as his true, cold focus bled into his expression for a split second.
"What do you want in return, Master Silas?" Kaelen asked, his voice flat, steady, and entirely devoid of his previous weakness.
Silas stared at the sudden shift in Kaelen's demeanor, a glint of fierce satisfaction flashing in his old eyes.
"I want Ironclad's arrogant little bastards to get their teeth kicked in," Silas rasped, turning back to his workbench with a wide, bitter grin. "Now put that chitin in the bin before the girl comes back."
